============================================================================================
GROUND-TRUTH RAW BYTES OBSERVED AT THE PROXY SIDECAR (over the network)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  V1 (4.1.129) residual -- the CRLF split FIRED on the wire:
    'CONNECT internal.local:1337 HTTP/1.1\r\nhost: internal.local:1337\r\n'
    'X-Forwarded-For: 1.2.3.4\r\nGET /flag?n=cmp HTTP/1.1\r\nX-Junk: 1\r\n\r\n'
    sidecar HIT -> "GET /flag?n=cmp HTTP/1.1\t<FLAG>"    (a real second request line split off)

  V2 (4.1.133) residual -- NO bytes serialized (validation blocked before any write):
    raw = ''    sidecar: (no hits)    app: ProxyConnectException / IllegalArgumentException

  This is the incomplete-fix core: CVE-2025-67735 added request-line validation (blocks the seed
  URI-CRLF on V1) and header-validation-by-default, BUT HttpProxyHandler.newInitialMessage()
  hard-coded withValidation(false) -> the SAME CRLF, moved into a CONNECT proxy header value,
  still splits the wire on V1. CVE-2026-42578 replaces the hard-coded false with
  validateInitialHeaders (default true), rejecting the value on V2.

============================================================================================
BENIGN / SAFEGUARD-SAFE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  The injected header carries a benign extra "GET /flag" request line + ${PCB_FLAG}; the real
  proxy sidecar merely logs the raw CONNECT bytes and detects the injected line. No Java object /
  gadget / deserialization / code execution -- the smuggled internal request line observed on the
  wire is the sole signal. No real attack.

